speechtext:
May I say to my hon. friend at once that parliament is all powerful to do whatever it wishes to do;

but when parliament has enacted a law which prescribes certain conditions and lays down certain obligations, members of parliament, like the public generally, are obliged to respect the law as it stands. My hon. friend has made to-night a number of statements which, if permitted to pass unnoticed, would convey an entirely wrong impression to the public. He intimated, for example, that we had a Civil Service Commission which, he had been told, parliament could not control. Neither he nor any other member of parliament was ever told any such thing. There is in the statutes a law which gives certain powers to the Civil Service Commission, and as long as that law remains there, members of parliament, the government itself, are bound by that law. Parliament can amend, or change the law when it wishes to do so; but until parliament decides to amend and change laws, they must be respected by members of parliament as well as by everyone else.
As regards the matter under discussion at the moment, parliament has declared in a very special way what the obligations are upon certain public servants filling positions of trust. If my hon. friend wishes to bring in an amendment to the statutes and hav; it discussed, to do away with that obligation, he has a right to bring his amendment here and have it discussed, and if parliament decides to alter the law, the law will be altered and conditions will be changed.

It seems to me that the hon. gentleman would like these boats to lie idle, that is with the machinery all going to waste and no men on them. Then, if accidents occurred at sea, before the ship could go to the aid of the people requiring it, months would elapse. When I hear the hon. member for West Toronto (Mr. Hocken) speaking, particularly when he rises with that air of self-righteousness and endeavours to scold the government or any department for .extravagance, I often wonder what his friends did when they were in power. In this regard, nothing has been done that is too much for the good sailors of the Maritime provinces and of the British Columbia coast. I will say, in conclusion, that my hon. friend reminds me very much of that old quotation, since we are in power and they are over there:
The Devil was sick, the Devil a monk would be;
The Devil was well, the Devil a monk was he.
